            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Multidimensional Roots of the 1857 Uprising</h3>\n<p>The monumental upheaval of 1857 was the explosive culmination of a century of accumulated Indian grievances spanning political, economic, religious, and military spheres:</p>\n\n<h3>2. Political and Imperial Causes</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Doctrine of Lapse:</strong> Lord Dalhousie's annexation of native states—Satara (1848), Jaitpur and Sambalpur (1849), Baghat (1850), Udaipur (1852), Jhansi (1853), and Nagpur (1854)—refused recognition to adopted heirs, sending shockwaves through every princely house in India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Annexation of Awadh (1856):</strong> Despite Nawab Wajid Ali Shah being a steadfast British ally, Dalhousie annexed Awadh citing 'gross misgovernance' based on James Outram's report. This dissolved the Awadh court, demobilized 60,000 royal soldiers, and alienated hundreds of powerful <em>taluqdars</em> (barons) whose estates were seized.</li>\n  <li><strong>Degradation of the Mughal Dynasty:</strong> Dalhousie announced that upon Bahadur Shah Zafar's death, the royal family would be evicted from the historic Red Fort. Lord Canning subsequently declared that Zafar's successors would not even be recognized as 'Kings' (Padshah), but merely as 'Princes'.</li>\n  <li><strong>Discontinuation of Nana Saheb's Pension:</strong> The British refused to continue the pension of 8 lakh rupees to Dhondu Pant (Nana Saheb), the adopted son of the last Maratha Peshwa Baji Rao II, turning him into a committed insurgent.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Socio-Religious Resentment &amp; Conversion Fears</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Aggressive Missionary Activity:</strong> The Charter Act of 1813 had permitted Christian missionaries to enter India. Missionaries publicly mocked Hindu gods and Islamic traditions in village markets and prisons. Government officials openly attended missionary preaching.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Lex Loci Act (Religious Disabilities Act of 1850):</strong> Under traditional Hindu and Islamic jurisprudence, an individual who renounced their faith forfeited ancestral inheritance. The 1850 Act abrogated this rule, allowing converts to Christianity to inherit family patrimony, creating deep alarm.</li>\n  <li><strong>Social Legislation as Cultural Encroachment:</strong> While modern historians view the Abolition of Sati (Regulation XVII of 1829) and the Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act (Act XV of 1856, drafted by Dalhousie and enacted by Canning) as progressive, the orthodox population perceived them as deliberate assaults by foreign infidels on age-old religious traditions.</li>\n</ol>\n",
