            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Economic Foundations of Discontent</h3>\n<p>The economic devastation wrought by the East India Company created a powder keg of societal anger:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Dispossession of the Awadh Taluqdars:</strong> Under the <em>Summary Settlement of 1856</em>, British revenue officers bypassed the traditional landed barons (taluqdars) of Awadh, stripping them of nearly half their ancestral estates and disarming their forts. When the 1857 revolt broke out, these taluqdars emerged as natural leaders of agrarian rebellion.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Inam Commission (1852):</strong> In Bombay Presidency, the Inam Commission scrutinized the title deeds of rent-free land grants. Because many families held lands by ancient customary tenure without written deeds, the Commission confiscated over <strong>20,000 estates</strong>, throwing prominent aristocratic families into destitution.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Military Discontent Among the Sepoys</h3>\n<p>The British Indian Army was overwhelming composed of Indian soldiers commanded by white British officers. Sepoys endured deep systemic humiliation:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Racial Discrimination &amp; Glass Ceiling:</strong> No matter how brave or senior an Indian soldier was, he could never be promoted above the rank of <strong>Subedar</strong> (in infantry) or <strong>Risaldar</strong> (in cavalry). A raw, eighteen-year-old British subaltern received higher pay and respect than a battle-hardened, elderly Indian Subedar.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Foreign Allowance (Batta):</strong> Sepoys received extra allowances (<em>batta</em>) when deployed in foreign territories. Once Sindh (1843) and Punjab (1849) were annexed into British India, the government abruptly cancelled their batta, causing mutinous unrest.</li>\n  <li><strong>The General Service Enlistment Act (1856):</strong> Enacted by Lord Canning, this law made it mandatory for all future Bengal Army recruits to serve wherever ordered, including overseas across the ocean. High-caste soldiers believed crossing the sea (<em>Kala Pani</em>) resulted in ritual pollution and permanent loss of caste.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The Immediate Spark: The Greased Cartridge</h3>\n<p>In late 1856, the military authorities replaced the obsolete, smoothbore <em>Brown Bess</em> musket with the advanced, rifled <strong>Enfield Rifle</strong>. The loading drill required soldiers to bite off the greased paper tip of the cartridge to expose the gunpowder before ramming it down the rifled barrel.</p>\n<p>In January 1857, a low-caste laborer (<em>khalasi</em>) at the Dum Dum ordnance factory near Calcutta reportedly told an upper-caste Brahmin sepoy that the grease applied to the new cartridges was composed of <strong>cow fat and pig lard</strong>. To a Hindu, the cow was sacred; to a Muslim, the pig was strictly prohibited (<em>haram</em>). The rumor spread like wildfire across Northern garrisons: soldiers concluded the British were orchestrating a systematic plot to defile and convert them to Christianity.</p>\n",
