            "title": "Outbreak: From Berhampore and Barrackpore to Meerut and Delhi (Bahadur Shah Zafar)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Early Tremors: Berhampore and Barrackpore</h3>\n<p>The explosive reaction to the Enfield greased cartridges began in early 1857 in Bengal:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Berhampore (26 February 1857):</strong> The <strong>19th Bengal Native Infantry</strong> refused to accept the percussion caps and cartridges. The regiment was marched to Barrackpore and formally disbanded in March.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mangal Pandey at Barrackpore (29 March 1857):</strong>\n    <p>On Sunday afternoon, 29 March, <strong>Mangal Pandey</strong>, a young Brahmin sepoy from Ballia (UP) belonging to the <strong>34th Bengal Native Infantry</strong>, walked onto the parade ground at Barrackpore in full uniform, carrying a loaded musket. Urging his fellow sepoys to defend their religion, he shot at Adjutant Lieutenant Baugh and attacked Sergeant-Major Hewson. When British troops approached, he attempted suicide rather than surrender.</p>\n    <p>Mangal Pandey was court-martialed and hanged on <strong>8 April 1857</strong>, becoming the celebrated first martyr of 1857. Jemadar Ishwari Prasad was executed on 21 April for refusing to order the guard to arrest Pandey. The 34th Regiment was disbanded on 6 May.</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Spark at Meerut (10 May 1857)</h3>\n<p>The definitive explosion erupted at the major military station of <strong>Meerut</strong>, 40 miles north-east of Delhi:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Defiance (24 April):</strong> When ordered to fire the new cartridges during parade, 85 out of 90 troopers of the <strong>3rd Native Cavalry</strong> refused.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Humiliation (9 May):</strong> The 85 troopers were court-martialed, publicly stripped of their uniforms, fettered with heavy iron shackles on the parade ground, and marched off to 10 years of rigorous imprisonment in Meerut jail.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Eruption (10 May 1857):</strong> The next day, Sunday evening, as British soldiers were preparing for church, the native troops of the 11th and 20th Native Infantry and 3rd Cavalry mutinied. They stormed the jail, smashed open cell doors, released their 85 comrades and hundreds of civilian prisoners, shot European officers, torched cantonment bungalows, and sounded the battle-cry: <em>'Maro Firangi Ko!'</em> (Strike down the foreigner!).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. March to Delhi and the Proclamation of Bahadur Shah Zafar</h3>\n<p>Through the dark night of 10 May, the Meerut cavalry galloped relentlessly along the Grand Trunk Road. Early on the morning of <strong>11 May 1857</strong>, they crossed the Yamuna bridge of boats into Delhi. They entered the <strong>Red Fort</strong>, surrounded the aged, bewildered Mughal Emperor <strong>Bahadur Shah Zafar</strong>, and pleaded for his royal blessing.</p>\n<p>Recognizing the historic magnitude of the crisis, the 82-year-old Emperor accepted leadership of the rebellion on 12 May. He was proclaimed <strong>Shahenshah-e-Hindustan</strong> (Emperor of India). Imperial firmans were dispatched across the subcontinent calling upon rajas, nawabs, taluqdars, and citizens to unite under the Mughal standard. With the fall of Delhi, what began as a cantonment mutiny was transformed into a full-scale revolutionary war of national independence.</p>\n",
