            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Delhi: The Imperial Capital &amp; General Bakht Khan</h3>\n<p>Although Bahadur Shah Zafar provided supreme symbolic and moral legitimacy, actual authority in Delhi was held by <strong>General Bakht Khan</strong>, who arrived from Bareilly in early July with thousands of rebel soldiers and artillery batteries:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Court of Administration:</strong> Bakht Khan created a revolutionary governing body known as the <em>Court of Administration</em> (consisting of 10 members: 6 from the military and 4 from the civil department). This council oversaw food supplies, collected revenues, preserved communal harmony between Hindus and Muslims (banning cow slaughter during Eid), and organized military defenses along the Delhi Ridge.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mughal Princes:</strong> Mirza Mughal was appointed commander-in-chief, but internal friction between the royal princes and Bakht Khan's disciplined veterans weakened coordinated defense.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Kanpur: The Peshwa's Revenge</h3>\n<p>At Kanpur, the rebellion was led by <strong>Nana Saheb</strong> (Dhondu Pant), who expelled General Sir Hugh Wheeler's British force and proclaimed himself <strong>Peshwa</strong>, acknowledging Bahadur Shah as Emperor:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Tatya Tope (Ramachandra Pandurang):</strong> Nana Saheb's peerless military lieutenant, a master of mobile guerrilla warfare and artillery tactics.</li>\n  <li><strong>Azimullah Khan:</strong> Nana Saheb's brilliant young prime minister and diplomat, who had visited London and the Crimean battlefields, managing revolutionary correspondence and publishing patriotic bulletins.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Tragedy of Kanpur:</strong> In late June 1857, Wheeler's besieged garrison surrendered at <em>Satichaura Ghat</em> under promise of safe passage to Allahabad. As boats departed, shooting erupted, killing most men. Surviving women and children were confined in the <em>Bibighar</em> and tragically executed before General Henry Havelock's advancing British relief column arrived, inciting ferocious British vengeance.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Lucknow: Begum Hazrat Mahal &amp; The Awadh War</h3>\n<p>Nowhere was the rebellion more widespread or deeply rooted in the peasantry than in Awadh, where the uprising was a genuine people's war:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Begum Hazrat Mahal:</strong> One of the greatest heroines of 1857. Defying the British, she proclaimed her 11-year-old son, <strong>Birjis Qadir</strong>, as Nawab of Awadh and established a national government at the Qaiserbagh palace.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inclusive Leadership:</strong> She united all factions—Hindus, Muslims, taluqdars, and sepoys. Raja Jailal Singh served as chief commander, and Mammu Khan was appointed chief minister.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Siege of the Residency:</strong> The British community, led by Chief Commissioner <strong>Sir Henry Lawrence</strong>, retreated into the fortified Lucknow Residency. On 2 July, an artillery shell struck Lawrence's room; he died on 4 July. The Residency endured a grueling multi-month siege until relieved by Sir Colin Campbell in March 1858. Begum Hazrat Mahal refused all British surrender offers and pensions, eventually finding refuge in Nepal.</li>\n</ul>\n",
