            "title": "Storm Centers: Jhansi (Rani Lakshmibai), Bareilly (Khan Bahadur Khan), Faizabad (Maulvi Ahmadullah)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi: The Heroine of 1857</h3>\n<p><strong>Manikarnika Tambe</strong> (born in Banaras, 1828) married Maharaja Gangadhar Rao of Jhansi. When the Maharaja died in 1853, Lord Dalhousie invoked the <strong>Doctrine of Lapse</strong>, refusing to recognize their adopted son, <strong>Damodar Rao</strong> (Anand Rao), and annexing the principality. The Rani famously declared: <em>'Main meri Jhansi nahi doongi!'</em> (I shall never give up my Jhansi!).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Defense of Jhansi (March 1858):</strong> When <strong>Major-General Sir Hugh Rose</strong> besieged Jhansi, the Rani commanded artillery defenses herself, aided by brave female fighters like <strong>Jhalkaribai</strong>. Tatya Tope marched to her aid with 20,000 men but was defeated at the Battle of the Betwa.</li>\n  <li><strong>Daring Escape &amp; Capture of Gwalior:</strong> Strapping 5-year-old Damodar Rao to her back, the Rani leaped over the city ramparts on her horse <em>Badal</em> and galloped 100 miles to Kalpi. Joining Tatya Tope and the Nawab of Banda, they executed a masterstroke: capturing the impregnable <strong>Gwalior Fort</strong> from the British ally, Maharaja Scindia.</li>\n  <li><strong>Martyrdom at Kotah-ki-Serai (17 June 1858):</strong> Fighting in male cavalry attire with dual swords, the Rani fell heroically near Gwalior. Sir Hugh Rose, her British adversary, wrote in admiration: <em>'The Indian Mutiny has produced but one man, and that man was a woman—the bravest and best military leader of the rebels.'</em></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Khan Bahadur Khan of Bareilly (Rohilkhand)</h3>\n<p>In Bareilly, the capital of Rohilkhand, the leadership was assumed by <strong>Khan Bahadur Khan</strong>, a descendant of the legendary Rohilla chief Hafiz Rahmat Khan. A former pensioner of the British, he raised an army of 40,000 Rohillas, appointed Hindu officers (like Shobha Ram as his chief revenue minister), and offered stiff resistance to Sir Colin Campbell until captured and executed in 1860.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah of Faizabad: 'The Danka Shah'</h3>\n<p>Originally hailing from Arcot (Tamil Nadu), <strong>Maulvi Ahmadullah Shah</strong>, known as <em>Danka Shah</em> (the Maulvi with the drum), was one of the most brilliant guerrilla strategists of 1857:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Battle of Chinhat (30 June 1857):</strong> Commanded rebel forces that decisively defeated British Chief Commissioner Sir Henry Lawrence at Chinhat, forcing the British into the Lucknow Residency.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Price on His Head:</strong> The British considered him their most dangerous enemy, placing an unprecedented reward of <strong>Rs. 50,000</strong> on his capture. On 5 June 1858, he was treacherously shot dead at the gates of Powayan by the brother of Raja Jagannath Singh, who severed his head and presented it to the British magistrate at Shahjahanpur to claim the blood money.</li>\n</ol>\n",
