            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Siege and Fall of Delhi (June – September 1857)</h3>\n<p>The British realized that to crush the rebellion, they must recapture Delhi, its symbolic political heart. British forces gathered on the <strong>Delhi Ridge</strong> under Generals Barnard and Wilson, reinforced by the Punjab Movable Column led by <strong>Brigadier-General John Nicholson</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Assault (14 September 1857):</strong> British, Sikh, and Gurkha troops blew up the <strong>Kashmiri Gate</strong> with explosives. Nicholson was shot in the chest during the street fighting and died nine days later. After six days of ferocious house-to-house urban combat, the British retook Delhi on 20 September.</li>\n  <li><strong>Atrocities at Khooni Darwaza:</strong> The elderly Emperor Bahadur Shah Zafar took sanctuary at <strong>Humayun's Tomb</strong>. On 21 September, <strong>Major William Hodson</strong> captured the Emperor. The next day, Hodson arrested the Emperor's sons, <em>Mirza Mughal</em> and <em>Mirza Khizr Sultan</em>, and grandson <em>Mirza Abu Bakr</em>. At the Delhi Gate (ever since known as <strong>Khooni Darwaza</strong>), Hodson ordered the princes to strip and shot them dead at point-blank range with his carbine.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Fate of Zafar:</strong> The Emperor was subjected to a show trial in the Red Fort, found guilty of rebellion, and deported to <strong>Rangoon (Burma)</strong> with Empress Zeenat Mahal, where he died an exile in November 1862. His poignant self-composed epitaph lamented: <em>'Kitna hai bad-naseeb Zafar dafn ke liye, do gaz zameen bhi na mili koo-e-yaar mein'</em> (How unfortunate is Zafar, who could not even get two yards of earth for his grave in his beloved homeland).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Colin Campbell &amp; The Reconquest of UP</h3>\n<p>In August 1857, <strong>Sir Colin Campbell</strong> arrived as the new Commander-in-Chief. Working alongside Generals <strong>Henry Havelock</strong> and <strong>James Outram</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Kanpur Recaptured:</strong> Campbell defeated Tatya Tope at the Second Battle of Kanpur in December 1857, securing the upper Gangetic communications.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lucknow Recaptured:</strong> In March 1858, Campbell assembled a colossal force of 30,000 British troops and 9,000 Gurkhas led by Maharaja Jung Bahadur of Nepal, finally overrunning Lucknow after fierce street battles at the Begum Kothi and Qaiserbagh.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rohilkhand Cleared:</strong> Campbell captured Bareilly in May 1858, forcing Khan Bahadur Khan to flee.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Colonial Terror &amp; The End of Tatya Tope</h3>\n<p>The suppression was characterized by terrifying racial savagery. <strong>Colonel James Neill</strong> at Allahabad hanged thousands of suspected villagers from roadside trees, burning entire settlements. Sepoys were routinely strapped across the mouths of cannons and blown to pieces ('blowing from guns').</p>\n<p>In Central India, <strong>Tatya Tope</strong> conducted a dazzling 10-month guerrilla campaign across Malwa, Rajasthan, and the Narmada valley, eluding seven British columns. In April 1859, he was betrayed by <strong>Raja Man Singh of Narwar</strong> while asleep in the Paron forests. Tatya Tope was tried by court-martial and hanged at <strong>Shivpuri</strong> on <strong>18 April 1859</strong>, drawing the final curtain on the great revolt.</p>\n",
