            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The End of Company Raj: Government of India Act 1858</h3>\n<p>The cataclysm of 1857 convinced the British Parliament that a private commercial corporation could no longer govern an empire of over 200 million people. On <strong>2 August 1858</strong>, the British Parliament enacted the historic <strong>Act for the Better Government of India (Government of India Act 1858)</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Direct Crown Rule:</strong> The sovereignty and territories of the East India Company were formally transferred to Queen Victoria and the British Crown. The rule of Company Raj, established at Plassey in 1757, ended exactly 101 years later.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Dual Government:</strong> The dual administrative machinery established by Pitt's India Act of 1784—the <em>Court of Directors</em> and the <em>Board of Control</em>—was abolished.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Secretary of State for India:</strong> All executive powers were vested in a new British cabinet minister titled the <strong>Secretary of State for India</strong>, stationed in London. <strong>Lord Stanley</strong> became the first Secretary of State. He was assisted by an advisory body of 15 members known as the <strong>Council of India</strong> (at least nine of whom had served in India for at least ten years).</li>\n  <li><strong>Creation of the Viceroy:</strong> The Governor-General of India was vested with the additional title of <strong>Viceroy</strong> (meaning the personal representative of the British monarch). <strong>Lord Canning</strong> became the first Viceroy of India.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Queen Victoria's Proclamation (1 November 1858)</h3>\n<p>On <strong>1 November 1858</strong>, Lord Canning held a magnificent imperial Durbar at <strong>Allahabad</strong> to read the historic Proclamation issued by Queen Victoria. Hailed by moderate Indian leaders as the <em>'Magna Carta of the Indian People'</em>, the Royal Proclamation made significant conciliation pledges:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>End of Annexations:</strong> Britain disclaimed any intention to extend its territorial possessions in India. The <strong>Doctrine of Lapse was permanently abolished</strong>, and native rulers were explicitly granted the right to adopt heirs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Religious Neutrality:</strong> The Crown pledged strict non-interference in the religious beliefs, customs, and worship of its Indian subjects, putting a brake on aggressive missionary activities and social reform legislation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Equal Opportunity in Public Service:</strong> Declared that all subjects, <em>'of whatever race or creed, be freely and impartially admitted to offices in our service'</em> on the basis of education, ability, and integrity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Royal Amnesty:</strong> Granted unconditional general pardon and amnesty to all rebels, mutineers, and civil insurgents, excepting only those who had directly participated in the murder of British subjects.</li>\n</ol>\n",
