                "Altered Troop Ratios: In the Bengal Army, the ratio of European to Indian troops was fixed at 1:2 (one European for every two Indians); in Bombay and Madras armies, it was fixed at 1:3.",
                "Monopoly over Strategic Artillery: Artillery, heavy ammunition, and sophisticated weaponry were placed exclusively in European hands; Indians were completely barred from artillery units (except a few mountain batteries).",
                "Invention of the 'Martial and Non-Martial Races' Doctrine: Communities that helped the British suppress 1857 (Sikhs, Gurkhas, Pathans, Punjabi Muslims, Baluchis) were designated 'Martial Races' and heavily recruited.",
                "Communities that spearheaded the 1857 revolt (Awadh Brahmins, Bhojpur Rajputs, Marathas, Bengalis, Biharis) were branded 'Non-Martial' and systematically weeded out of the army.",
                "Divide and Rule: Communal and caste segregation was enforced within regiments; soldiers were encouraged to view fellow soldiers through caste and regional prejudices.",
                "Civil Policy: The British abandoned active social reform and entered into an unholy alliance with conservative feudal elements (zamindars, taluqdars, and princes), treating them as bulwarks of imperial security."
