            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Poligar Wars of Tamil Nadu (1799–1805)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Poligars</strong> (<em>Palayakkars</em>) were hereditary feudal chieftains and military governors of South India:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Veerapandiya Kattabomman:</strong> The legendary Poligar of Panchalankurichi (Tirunelveli) refused to pay extortionate kisti to the Company collector, Colin Jackson. Kattabomman fortified his palace and fought heroically. He was betrayed by the Raja of Pudukkottai, captured, and publicly hanged from a tamarind tree at <strong>Kayathar</strong> on 16 October 1799.</li>\n  <li><strong>The South Indian Confederacy &amp; Marudu Brothers:</strong> In 1800–1801, the <strong>Marudu Pandyan brothers</strong> (Periya Marudu and Chinna Marudu) of Sivaganga organized an all-South India military alliance uniting poligars of Tamil Nadu, Kerala (Kerala Varma Pazhassi Raja), and Karnataka (Dhundia Wagh).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Tiruchirappalli Proclamation (June 1801):</strong> Chinna Marudu issued a historic proclamation pasted on the walls of the Srirangam temple and the Nawab's palace at Tiruchi, calling upon all Hindus, Muslims, and castes to unite in holy war to expel the British. The rebellion was crushed with severe brutality; 73 leaders were hanged at Tiruppathur fort in October 1801.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Rani Chennamma of Kittur (1824)</h3>\n<p>Decades before the Revolt of 1857, <strong>Rani Chennamma</strong> of Kittur (Belgaum, Karnataka) rose in defiance when British political agent St. John Thackeray invoked an early prototype of the Doctrine of Lapse to disallow her adopted son, Shivalingappa, and annex Kittur. Chennamma's forces killed Thackeray and held the fort. Defeated by heavy British reinforcements in December 1824, Chennamma was imprisoned at Bailhongal fort, where she died in 1829. Her loyal commander, <strong>Sangolli Rayanna</strong>, continued a heroic guerrilla struggle until his capture and execution in 1831.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Ramosi Uprisings &amp; The Kuka Movement</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Ramosi Rebellions (Maharashtra):</strong> The Ramosis, hill tribes who served as watchmen and scouts under the Peshwas, were left destitute after the British annexed the Maratha territories in 1818. Led by <strong>Chittur Singh</strong> (1822) and later the legendary outlaw <strong>Umaji Naik</strong> (1826–1831), they raided government treasuries and ambushed British convoys until Umaji was betrayed and hanged in 1832.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Kuka (Namdhari) Movement of Punjab:</strong> Founded around 1840 by <strong>Bhagat Jawahar Mal</strong> and <strong>Baba Balak Singh</strong> as a religious purification movement within Sikhism. Under <strong>Baba Ram Singh</strong> (1816–1885), it evolved into a potent political movement aiming to overthrow British rule and restore the Sikh kingdom.\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Anticipating Gandhi:</em> Baba Ram Singh organized the Kukas into a parallel government with 22 <em>Subas</em> (governors), an independent postal dispatch (<em>Dawk</em>), and enforced total boycott of British schools, courts, railways, and foreign clothing.</li>\n      <li><em>The Malerkotla Tragedy (1872):</em> Following clashes over cow slaughter at Malerkotla, British Deputy Commissioner Cowan tied 65 captured Kukas to artillery cannons and blew them to pieces without trial. Baba Ram Singh was arrested and exiled to Rangoon, where he died in 1885.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n",
