            "title": "Other Tribal Uprisings: Kol Uprising, Khond Resistance (Chakra Bisoi), Bhil Revolts, Rampa Rebellion",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Kol Uprising of Chota Nagpur (1831–1832)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Kols</strong> (including Ho, Munda, and Oraon inhabitants) of Chota Nagpur rose in a massive insurrection in 1831:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Causes:</strong> The Raja of Chota Nagpur leased out ancestral tribal villages to alien Muslim, Sikh, and North Indian <em>Thikadars</em>, who dispossessed the tribal headmen (<em>Mankis</em>) and extorted illegal taxes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Leadership:</strong> Led by <strong>Buddho Bhagat</strong>, Madara Mahato, and Bindrai Manki. Buddho Bhagat died fighting heroically on the battlefield with his sons and 150 followers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Administrative Outcome:</strong> Captain Thomas Wilkinson suppressed the rebellion and recognized the need for special administrative machinery, leading to <strong>Regulation XIII of 1833</strong>, which established the <strong>South-Western Frontier Agency (SWFA)</strong> with headquarters at Kishanpur (Ranchi).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Khond Uprisings &amp; The Meriah Issue (1837–1856)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Khonds</strong> inhabited the hilly tracts extending from Odisha to the Srikakulam districts of Andhra Pradesh:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Meriah Controversy:</strong> The British colonial administration intervened in Khond customary religious practices by attempting to suppress <em>Meriah</em> (traditional human sacrifice performed to ensure fertility of the turmeric crop). The British established a dedicated <em>Meriah Agency</em> under Captain Campbell.</li>\n  <li><strong>Leadership of Chakra Bisoi:</strong> Resenting foreign interference in their religion alongside heavy forest taxes, the Khonds rallied under the young chieftain <strong>Chakra Bisoi</strong>. For nearly two decades, Chakra Bisoi waged an elusive guerrilla campaign through the dense Eastern Ghats, disappearing into the forests without ever being captured.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. The Bhil Rebellions &amp; Tantia Bhil</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Bhils</strong>, residing in Khandesh, Rajasthan, and Gujarat, revolted repeatedly between 1818 and 1846 under <strong>Sewaram</strong> against British East India Company occupation. In the late 19th century, <strong>Tantia Bhil (Tantia Mama)</strong> emerged as an immortal folk hero in Madhya Pradesh, robbing British treasuries and wealthy moneylenders to distribute food to impoverished tribals, earning the title <em>'Robin Hood of India'</em> before being captured and hanged in 1889.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Alluri Sitarama Raju &amp; The Rampa Rebellion (1922–1924)</h3>\n<p>In the Rampa hill tract of Godavari (Andhra Pradesh), the British enacted the oppressive <strong>Madras Forest Act of 1882</strong>, which banned shifting cultivation (<em>Podu</em>) and criminalized the collection of minor forest produce:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Ascetic Leader:</strong> <strong>Alluri Sitarama Raju</strong>, an educated young sanyasi inspired by Mahatma Gandhi's Non-Cooperation Movement, mobilized the Koya and Konda Dora tribals. While preaching temperance and khadi, Raju concluded that only armed force could liberate India from British tyranny.</li>\n  <li><strong>Guerrilla Raids:</strong> Between 1922 and 1924, Raju led daring raids on British police stations at Chintapalle, Krishnadevipeta, and Rampachodavaram, capturing firearms while leaving signed notes detailing the seized ammunition.</li>\n  <li><strong>Martyrdom:</strong> The British deployed the Assam Rifles. Raju was captured at Koyyuru and shot dead while tied to a tree on <strong>7 May 1924</strong>. He is celebrated throughout Andhra Pradesh as <strong>Manyam Veerudu</strong> (Hero of the Jungles).</li>\n</ul>\n",
