            "title": "Agrarian Struggles in Bengal & Maharashtra: Pabna Agrarian League (1873) & Bengal Tenancy Act 1885",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Pabna Peasant Uprising (1873–1876)</h3>\n<p>Following the Permanent Settlement of 1793, zamindars possessed near-total arbitrary power to evict tenants and extract illegal cesses (<em>abwabs</em>). In the early 1870s, zamindars in East Bengal attempted to prevent ryots from acquiring permanent occupancy rights under Act X of 1859 by forcibly altering tenancy records and raising rents exponentially:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Formation of the Agrarian League (May 1873):</strong> In Yusufshahi pargana of <strong>Pabna district</strong>, peasants formed an <strong>Agrarian League</strong>. The movement was spearheaded by <strong>Ishan Chandra Roy</strong> (affectionately hailed by the villagers as <em>Bidrohi Raja</em>), <strong>Shambhu Pal</strong>, and <strong>Khoodi Mollah</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Constitutional Nature of the Struggle:</strong> Unlike tribal jacqueries, the Pabna movement was remarkably peaceful and legalistic. The League established branches in every village, raised subscriptions to hire the best lawyers, and boycotted payment of enhanced rents. Whenever zamindars filed eviction lawsuits, the League fought back in the civil courts and won.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Royalist Slogan:</strong> The Pabna ryots coined the clever constitutional slogan:\n    <blockquote><em>\"We want to be the ryots of Her Majesty the Queen of England and of Her only!\"</em></blockquote>\n    By appealing to Queen Victoria's sovereignty, they demanded the abolition of private zamindari intermediaries and conversion of their tenures into direct government holdings.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Nationalist Support &amp; The Bengal Tenancy Act of 1885</h3>\n<p>The young, radical leadership of the <strong>Indian Association</strong> (founded in 1876 by <strong>Surendranath Banerjea</strong>, Anandamohan Bose, and Dwarkanath Ganguly) organized mass rallies of over 20,000 peasants across Bengal to demand legal protection for tenant farmers. Thinkers like <strong>R.C. Dutt</strong> and <strong>Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay</strong> (in his essays on <em>Bangadesher Krishak</em>) championed peasant rights.</p>\n<p>Alarmed by the growing politicization of the countryside, Governor-General Lord Dufferin's administration enacted the landmark <strong>Bengal Tenancy Act of 1885</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Occupancy Rights:</strong> Any tenant who held land continuously in the same village for <strong>12 years</strong> was legally recognized as a 'Settled Ryot', acquiring permanent occupancy rights.</li>\n  <li><strong>Protection Against Eviction:</strong> An occupancy ryot could not be evicted except through a formal court decree for persistent non-payment of rent.</li>\n  <li><strong>Restraint on Rent Hikes:</strong> Zamindars were legally barred from raising rents arbitrarily; rents could only be increased on proven grounds of enhanced soil productivity or rising market prices, subject to strict judicial limits.</li>\n</ol>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Pabna Agrarian League (1873): Formed in Yusufshahi pargana of Pabna district (East Bengal) to resist illegal zamindari rent hikes and evictions.",
                "Leaders: Ishan Chandra Roy (Bidrohi Raja), Shambhu Pal, and Khoodi Mollah.",
                "Character of the Movement: Highly disciplined, legalistic, and non-violent; peasants raised funds, hired lawyers, challenged zamindars in British civil courts, and refused to pay enhanced rents.",
                "Famous Peasant Slogan: 'We want to be the ryots of Her Majesty Queen Victoria and of her only' (expressing desire to eliminate parasitic zamindars in favor of direct crown tenancy).",
                "Support from Nationalists: Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay, R.C. Dutt, and the Indian Association founded by Surendranath Banerjea and Anandamohan Bose vigorously supported the Pabna ryots.",
                "Legislative Triumph: The prolonged struggle led directly to the enactment of the landmark Bengal Tenancy Act of 1885.",
                "Provisions of Bengal Tenancy Act 1885: Granted occupancy rights to ryots who held land continuously for 12 years in a village, protected them against arbitrary eviction, and restricted zamindars from arbitrarily increasing rents.",
                "Deccan Agrarian Unrest (1875): Parallel peasant movement in Maharashtra against Marwari moneylenders; led to the Deccan Agriculturists' Relief Act 1879."
            ],
